== 4. The Best Men’s Sins

The fourth device that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is to present to the soul the best men’s sins and hide from the soul their virtues; to show the soul their sins and hide from the soul their sorrows and repentance: as by setting before the soul the adultery of David, the pride of Hezekiah, the impatience of Job, the drunkenness of Noah, the blasphemy of Peter, etc., and by hiding from the soul the tears, the sighs, the groans, the meltings, humblings and repentings of these precious souls.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of the devil are as follows.]

The first remedy against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that the Spirit of the Lord has been as careful to record the saints’ rising out of sin by repentance as he has to record their falling into sin. David falls fearfully, but by repentance he rises sweetly: “Blot out my transgressions. Wash me thoroughly of my iniquity and cleanse me from my sin. For I acknowledge my transgressions, and my sin is always before me. Purify me with hyssop, and I will be clean; wash me, and I will be whiter than snow. Deliver me from bloodguilt, O God, the God of my salvation.” It is true that Hezekiah’s heart was lifted up under the abundance of blessings that God had poured in on him; and it is as true that Hezekiah#footnote[2 Chr 32:25, 26.] “humbled the pride of his heart—he and the people of Jerusalem—so that the wrath of the #smallcaps[Lord] did not come upon them during the days of Hezekiah.” It is true that Job curses the day of his birth; and it is as true that he rises by repentance. “Behold, I am vile,” he says. “How can I reply to You?#footnote[Job 40:4, 5; 42:5, 6.] I place my hand over my mouth. I have spoken once, but I have no answer—twice, but I have nothing to add.” “My ears had heard of You, but now my eyes have seen You. Therefore I abhor myself,#footnote[Tertullian says that he was #emph[(Nulli rei natus nisi poenitentiae)] born for no other purpose but to repent.] and I repent in dust and ashes.” Peter falls dreadfully, but rises sweetly by repentance. A look of love from Christ melts him into tears; he knew that repentance was the key to the kingdom of grace. As once his faith was so great that he leaped, as it were, into a sea of waters to come to Christ, so now his repentance was so great that he leaped, as it were, into a sea of tears, because#footnote[Luther confesses that before his conversion he did not meet with a more displeasing word in all his study of theology than “repent”; but afterward he took delight in the work (#emph[poenitens de peccato dolet & de dolore gaudet]): to sorrow for his sin, and then to rejoice in his sorrow.] he had gone from Christ. Some say that after his sad fall he was weeping again and again, and that his face was even furrowed with continual tears. No sooner had he taken in poison than he vomited it up again, before it reached his vital parts. No sooner had he handled this serpent than he turned it into a rod to scourge his soul with remorse for sinning against such clear light, such strong love, and such sweet revelations of the heart of Christ to him. Clement notes that Peter so repented that all his life afterward, every night when he heard the rooster crow, he would fall on his knees and, weeping bitterly, beg pardon for this sin. Ah, souls! You#super[+] can easily sin like the saints, but can you#super[+] repent with the saints? Many can sin with David and Peter who cannot repent with David and Peter, and so must perish forever. The emperor Theodosius, pressing to receive the Lord’s Supper, excused#footnote[Theodoret, #emph[Ecclesiastical History,] book 5, chapter 17.] his own foul deed by David’s having done the like. To this Ambrose replied, “You have followed David transgressing; follow David repenting, and then think of the table of the Lord.”

The second remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that these saints did not make a trade of sin. They fell once or twice (and rose by repentance) so that they might#footnote[The saints cannot sin (#emph[voluntate plenâ sed semiplena]) with a whole will, but as it were with half a will, an unwilling willingness; not with a full consent, but with a dissenting consent.] keep the closer to Christ forever. They fell incidentally, on some occasion, and with much reluctance; but you sin presumptuously, obstinately, readily, delightfully and habitually. By making a trade of sin you have brought on your soul a kind of cursed necessity of sinning, so that you could as well cease to be, or cease to live, as cease to sin. Sin has become by custom like a second nature to you, which you cannot, which you will not, lay aside, though you know that if you do not lay sin aside, God will lay your soul aside forever; though you know that if sin and your soul do not part, Christ and your soul can never meet. Will you make a trade of sin, and cry out, “Did not David sin like this, and Noah#footnote[2 Pet 2:14. Prov 4:14, 16. Though sin does (#emph[habitare]) dwell in the regenerate, as Augustine notes, yet it does not (#emph[regnare]) reign over the regenerate; they rise by repentance.] sin like this, and Peter sin like this, #emph[etc.?]” No! Their hearts turned aside to folly one day, but your heart turns aside to folly every day. And when they had fallen, they rose by repentance, and by the actings of faith on a crucified Christ; but you fall, and have neither strength nor will to rise, but wallow in sin, and will eternally die in your sins, unless the Lord is the more merciful to your soul. Do you think, O soul, that this is good reasoning: “Such a one tasted poison only once, and yet narrowly escaped; but I drink poison daily, and yet I will escape”? Yet such is the mad reasoning of worldly souls: “David and Peter, #emph[etc.,] sinned once, foully and fearfully; they tasted poison only once, and were sick to death; but I taste it daily, and yet I will not taste eternal death.” Remember, O souls, that the day is at hand when self-flatterers will be found self-deceivers, indeed, self-murderers.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that though God does not (and never will) disinherit his people for their sins, yet he has severely punished his people for their sins. David sins, and God breaks his bones for his sin: “Let me#footnote[Ps 51:8. Josephus reports that not long after the Jews had crucified Christ on the cross, so many of them were condemned to be crucified that there were not places enough for crosses, nor crosses enough for the bodies that were to be hung on them.] hear joy and gladness; let the bones You have broken rejoice.” “And because you have done this, the sword will not depart from your house to the day of your death.” Though God will not utterly take his loving devotion from them, nor allow his faithfulness to fail, nor break his covenant, nor alter what has gone out of his mouth, yet he will punish their transgressions with the rod, and their iniquity with stripes (Ps 89:30, 35). Scripture abounds with instances of this kind. This is so well-known a truth, among all who know anything of truth, that to cite more Scriptures to prove it would be to light a candle to see the sun at noon. The Jews have a proverb that “There is no punishment that comes on Israel in which there is not an ounce of the golden calf”; meaning that it was so great a sin that God remembered it in every plague, and that it had an influence on every trouble that befell them. Every man’s heart may say to him in his sufferings, as the heart of Apollodorus#footnote[ἐγώ σοι τούτων αἰτία.] in the cauldron said, “I have been the cause of this.” God is most angry when he shows no anger. God keep me from this mercy! This kind of mercy is worse than#footnote[#emph[Qui non est cruciatus non est christianus,] says Luther: there is not a Christian who does not carry his cross.] all other misery. One, writing to a sick friend, has this expression: “I count it a part of unhappiness not to know adversity. I judge you to be miserable, because you have not been miserable.” It is mercy that our affliction is not an execution but a correction. He who has deserved hanging may be glad if he escapes with a whipping. God’s corrections are our instructions, his lashes our lessons, his#footnote[Ps 94:12. Prov 3:12, 13; 6:23. Isa 26:9.] scourges our schoolmasters, his chastisements our warnings. And to note this, both the Hebrews and the Greeks express chastening and teaching#footnote[מוסר Παιδεία.] by one and the same word (#emph[musar, paideia]), because the latter is the true#footnote[Job 36:8, 9, 10.] end of the former, according to the proverb, “Smarting makes wise, and vexation gives understanding.” That is why Luther fitly calls affliction “the Christian man’s theology.”#footnote[Theologiam christianorum.] So says Job 33:14–18: “For God speaks in one way and in another, yet no one notices. In a dream, in a vision in the night, when deep sleep falls upon men as they slumber on their beds, He opens#footnote[#emph[Afflictiones benedictiones.] Bernard. Afflictions are blessings.] the ears of men and seals their instruction, to turn a man from wrongdoing and keep him from pride, to preserve his soul from the Pit and his life from perishing by the sword.” When Satan tells you of other men’s sins to draw you to sin, then think of the same men’s sufferings to keep you from sin. Lay your hand on your heart and say, “O my soul, if you sin with David, you must suffer with David, etc.”

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that there are only two main ends of God’s recording the falls of his saints. The one is to keep from fainting,#footnote[“I have known a good man,” said Bernard, “who, when he heard of anyone who had committed some notorious sin, used to say to himself (#emph[ille hodie & ego cras]), ‘He fell today; so may I tomorrow.’”] sinking and despair, under the burden of their sins, those who fall through weakness and infirmity. The other is that their falls may be like landmarks, to warn others who stand to take heed lest they fall. It never entered into the heart of God to record his children’s sins so that others might be encouraged to sin, but so that others might be warned to look to their standing, to cling the more tightly to the hem of Christ’s robe, and to avoid all occasions and temptations that may lead the soul to fall as others have fallen. When they have been left by Christ, the Lord has made their sins like landmarks, to warn his people to take heed how they come near those sands and rocks, those snares and baits, that have been fatal to the choicest treasures, namely, the joy, peace, comfort and glorious enjoyments, of the finest spirits and noblest souls that ever sailed through the ocean of this sinful, troublesome world, as you may see in David, Job, Peter, etc. There is nothing in the world that can so notoriously thwart the grand end of God’s recording the sins of his saints as for anyone to take encouragement from it to sin. And wherever you find such a soul, you may write him down as Christless, graceless, a soul cast off by God, a soul that Satan has by the hand; and the eternal God knows where he will lead him.
